    <exercise xml:id="ex-matching-skew-lines-to-angles">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-matching-skew-lines-to-angles" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/09-three-dimensional-geometry/02-angle-between-skew-lines/Matching-skew-lines-to-angles.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-angle-between-skew-lines">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-skew-angles-in-a-box">
            <statement><p>A cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m> has <m>AB = 20\,\text{cm}</m>, <m>BC = 15\,\text{cm}</m> and <m>AE = 8\,\text{cm}</m>. Find the angle between
                (a) <m>AB</m> and <m>CG</m>; (b) <m>AB</m> and <m>DG</m>; (c) <m>EH</m> and <m>BG</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>90^{\circ}</m> (b) <m>21.8^{\circ}</m> (c) <m>28.1^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>(a) Slide <m>AB</m> to <m>DC</m>, which meets <m>CG</m> at a right angle: <m>90^{\circ}</m>.</p>
                <p>(b) Slide <m>AB</m> to <m>DC</m>, which meets <m>DG</m> at <m>D</m>. Angle <m>CDG</m>: <m>\tan CDG = \frac{CG}{DC} = \frac{8}{20}</m>, so <m>21.8^{\circ}</m>.</p>
                <p>(c) <m>EH</m> is parallel to <m>FG</m>, which meets <m>BG</m> at <m>G</m>. Angle <m>BGF</m>: <m>\tan BGF = \frac{BF}{FG} = \frac{8}{15}</m>, so <m>28.1^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-skew-angles-in-a-cube">
            <statement><p><m>ABCDEFGH</m> is a cube. Find the angle between <m>AB</m> and <m>CH</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>45^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>AB</m> is parallel to <m>DC</m>, which meets <m>CH</m> at <m>C</m>. Angle <m>DCH</m> is between a side and a diagonal of the square face <m>DCGH</m>, so it is <m>45^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-skew-angles-rectangular-pyramid">
            <statement><p>A right pyramid <m>VABCD</m> stands on a rectangular base with <m>AB = 16\,\text{cm}</m> and <m>BC = 12\,\text{cm}</m>. Every sloping edge is <m>14\,\text{cm}</m>. Find the angle between
                (a) <m>VA</m> and <m>BC</m>; (b) <m>VA</m> and <m>CD</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>64.6^{\circ}</m> (b) <m>55.2^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>(a) <m>BC</m> is parallel to <m>AD</m> (<m>12\,\text{cm}</m>), which meets <m>VA</m> at <m>A</m>. In the isosceles face <m>VAD</m>, <m>\cos VAD = \frac{6}{14}</m>, so angle <m>VAD = 64.6^{\circ}</m>.</p>
                <p>(b) <m>CD</m> is parallel to <m>AB</m> (<m>16\,\text{cm}</m>). In the isosceles face <m>VAB</m>, <m>\cos VAB = \frac{8}{14}</m>, so angle <m>VAB = 55.2^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-classroom-skew-angle">
            <statement><p>A classroom is a cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m> with <m>AB = 9\,\text{m}</m>, <m>BC = 7\,\text{m}</m> and <m>AE = 3\,\text{m}</m>. Find the angle between the floor edge <m>AD</m> and the diagonal <m>CF</m> of the end wall <m>BCGF</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>23.2^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>AD</m> is parallel to <m>BC</m>, which meets <m>CF</m> at <m>C</m>. In the end wall, triangle <m>BCF</m> has a right angle at <m>B</m>: <m>\tan BCF = \frac{BF}{BC} = \frac{3}{7}</m>, so angle <m>BCF = 23.2^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-perpendicular-skew-lines">
            <statement><p>Explain why, in any cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m>, the angle between the vertical edge <m>AE</m> and any line in the base that does not pass through <m>A</m> is <m>90^{\circ}</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Slide <m>AE</m> to the vertical line through a point of the base line; a vertical line is perpendicular to every line in the horizontal base through its foot.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-perpendicular-face-diagonals">
            <title>Challenge: Perpendicular Face Diagonals</title>
            <statement><p><m>ABCDEFGH</m> is a cube. Show that the face diagonals <m>AF</m> and <m>CH</m> are perpendicular.</p></statement>
            <hint><p><m>AF</m> is parallel to <m>DG</m>.</p></hint>
            <answer><p>The angle between them is <m>90^{\circ}</m>.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>Slide <m>AF</m>, without turning it, to <m>DG</m>, the diagonal of the opposite face that slopes the same way. <m>DG</m> and <m>CH</m> are the two diagonals of the square <m>DCGH</m>, and the diagonals of a square cross at right angles. So the angle between <m>AF</m> and <m>CH</m> is <m>90^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-angle-between-skew-lines">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Use a model box and a pencil throughout. Give a step card: <q>slide, meet, triangle, tan</q>. Work through <xref ref="example-skew-lines-in-a-cuboid"/> and <xref ref="example-skew-lines-in-a-pyramid"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-skew-angles-in-a-box"/> and <xref ref="ex-skew-angles-in-a-cube"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity plus all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-skew-angles-in-a-box"/> to <xref ref="ex-perpendicular-skew-lines"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-perpendicular-face-diagonals"/>. Extension: in a cube <m>ABCDEFGH</m>, find the angle between <m>AG</m> and <m>BD</m>. (Answer: <m>90^{\circ}</m>. The projection of <m>AG</m> on the base is <m>AC</m>, which is perpendicular to <m>BD</m>, and <m>CG</m> is perpendicular to the base, so <m>BD</m> is perpendicular to the whole plane <m>ACGE</m>, which contains <m>AG</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

